A||||7||Formula88||Emissions & EGR||||10||
Z||000000||Formula88||04-05-2001||09:32 AM||||Ok, my EGR tube is cracked, but since I needed to get an emissions inspection and didn't have the money for a new pipe, yet, I just blocked off the port on the plenum and left the tube in place for the visual inspection.  The results?<P>Gas       Reading        Max Allowed<BR>=====================================<BR>HC(ppm)      040              220<BR>CO(%)       0.27             1.20<BR>CO2(%)     15.7<P>Not bad for a 109K mile Formula with a blocked off EGR, huh?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I've read about the dangers of not having the EGR hooked up, so I plan on fixing it ASAP.  I've also read about the alternatives to the factory pipe:  braided steel, 2.8 S10 or Firebird pipes, etc.  Who's tried some of the alternative fixes, and this is the important part, had it on there long enough to know if it's going to be a durable replacement.  Anything that'll fit might work for 6 months.  I want to fix it permanently (within reason).  It seems to me, since the flanges on both ends are loose, it wouldn't be difficult to fab a pipe myself and save the money.  I haven't tried this, of course.  Has anyone done it, yet?||||reg||10
Z||000001||1FST2M6||04-05-2001||09:53 AM||||as long as they don't check NOx (Oxides of Nitrogen) on their sniffer machine you'll be ok. with a working EGR you'll be around 13-2100ppm with a blocked off you easily be in the 18,000+ range. (my 2 cars both failed NOx with the EGR disconected)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||04-05-2001||09:55 AM||||Didn't think about that.  I don't know if they test for that here depending on the model year.  I'll check when I get my '99 Grand Am inspected.  Still, I want to fix the EGR so I don't burn a piston like I've heard so many people talk about.  Still, I was impressed with how clean the car ran.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||04-05-2001||10:03 AM||||Some states do not require NOx readings for cars prior to 1994. I think that is when the government stated the actual level of NOx.<BR>Here in ILL it is like that.||||reg||1
Z||000004||rodmcneill||04-05-2001||11:37 AM||||My 86 GT has had the EGR tube removed-blocked for 2 years now, no problems. I run mid-range gas and have not advanced the timing,no pinging. Does the ECM actually lean out the mixture when the EGR is working? I may also run one heat range cooler plugs. All the older cars and also street rods don't have EGR I guess all this talk about melting pistons is just that...talk. I don't drive my vehicles hard no stoplight to stoplight racing so this may be keeping the stress-heat level down.||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||04-05-2001||11:47 AM||||i drive my cars like their stolen. and i've got no problems with the EGR off... just emissions... that only takes 10 seconds to reattach the plugged vacuum tube. Iv'e had my EGrs not working for over 3 years and no adverse engine issues.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||04-05-2001||12:29 PM||||That sounds good.  I know old cars didn't have them, but if the ECM thinks there's EGR and tunes the engine accordingly, that's where the potential problem lies.  I'll keep a close check on my plugs to see if its running hot.  <P>Yea, I drive mine like it's stolen too.  To paraphrase that wonderful scene from Action Jackson, "Even though [my Fiero] is my pride and joy, it should always be driven as fast as humanly possible!" ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||04-05-2001||07:12 PM||||The EGR will only have an effect if the emissions are tested on a dyno. Even then only if they test under conditions that would make the ECM open the EGR. <P>EGR should never open at idle so it won't have an effect on testing at idle if it doesn't work. At that point you are only worried there is no exhaust/vac leak.||||reg||1
